In 1991, McLaren announced their first true road car. It was conceived by Gordon Murray who designed F1 machines for McLaren at the time, while its exterior design was created by Peter Stevens who designed the new shape of the Lotus Esprit. The car was called the 'F1'.

The monocoque body formed in carbon composites is extremely light, weighing just <180kg>, and the total weight of the car is only <1140kg>. The engine is a 6.1 L V12 made by BMW motorsports, and produces <636ps> and <66.4kgm> torque. It marked an official record speed of <391.0km/h> in 1998.

With high demands from the racing world, McLaren went on to develop a model with further refined aerodynamics: the McLaren F1 GTR. Delivery of this car started in 1994. From 1995 the McLaren F1 GTR entered the BPR series and won the season, but they also had another significant victory that year in winning the 24 Hours of Le Mans. In wet conditions that lasted almost all day long, the McLaren held back the open prototypes that were then said to have the advantage, and car #59 (which was practically a McLaren works car), driven by Yannick Dalmas, JJ Lehto and Masanori Sekiya, took the overall win.}
